--- Returns the window that had the keyboard, to give the keyboard back to
--- it. A view that a frontend draws itself (the arrange view, the mixer and
--- other views) also stays where it is. The separate window is a second
--- view of it. A view with a state for each window leaves only the pane
--- `pane` that it goes out of: its other windows stay.
---@param name any
---@param pane any
function paw.canvas.leave(name, pane) end

--- Show a view that paints in a graphical frontend, and give it the
--- keyboard. When the view has a place in the layout (`paw.ui.dock`, or a
--- window that shows it), it is docked, as the terminal shows it. If not,
--- or with `window = true`, it is in a separate window. A view that is
--- already in a separate window stays there. `opts`: `title`, `width`,
--- `height` (the start size of a window), `window`.
---@param name any
---@param opts any
function paw.canvas.open(name, opts) end

--- Show a view in a window of a graphical frontend (the focused view when
--- `name` is nil), and give it the keyboard. A view that paints is painted
--- (`M.open`). Other views are drawn as the terminal draws them, in cells of
--- the monospace font of the window, at the size of the window. A line
--- below the view shows prompts, messages and its keys. A view that floats
--- over the views of the terminal moves into the window. `opts`: `title`,
--- `width`, `height` (in pixels).
---   paw.canvas.open_view("undotree")
---@param name any
---@param opts any
function paw.canvas.open_view(name, opts) end

--- The views a frontend can offer to open in a window: those that take the
--- keyboard, `{ name, title }` each, by title. A view keeps itself off the
--- list with `window = false`. A view with a painted twin (`painted = "name"`
--- of a view that has `paint`) is offered as that one.
function paw.canvas.openable() end

--- Paint a view at a size. Returns the drawing as a list of operations:
---   { "r", x, y, w, h, fill, stroke, stroke width, radius }
---   { "t", x, y, text, colour, size, bold (0 or 1), align, width }
---   { "p", path commands, stroke, stroke width, fill }
---   { "m", x, y, w, h, text, colour, background, attributes }
---   { "g", x, y, size, codepoint, colour, font }   a music font's glyph
---   { "cut", x, y0, y1 }                           where a slice may end
---   { "layer", name }                              what follows is that layer
--- The attributes are a sum of 1 bold, 4 italic, 8 underline and 32 strike.
--- It also returns the title of the window, which a view can change.
--- 
--- The options are these. `cell` is the size `{ w, h }` of a cell of the
--- monospace font of the window (or of the terminal, for a surface).
--- `fresh` is true when the window has nothing yet. `glyphs` is true when
--- the frontend draws glyph operations itself. If not, they come as paths,
--- without the markers.
--- 
--- `surface` is true when the drawing goes into the cells of a terminal. Then the view does not draw its background and the
--- data that the bars of the terminal show. `colors` is `{ fg, bg }`, the
--- colors of the terminal.
--- 
--- `bars` comes from a frontend that shows a view
--- in a separate window, away from the bottom of its main window. The view
--- then draws the status line and the command line below it. The `paint`
--- function of the view gets these in its context, as `cell`, `surface`,
--- `fg` and `bg`. A view that draws cells gives `{ same = true }` when
--- nothing changes.
---@param name any
---@param width any
---@param height any
---@param opts any
function paw.canvas.paint(name, width, height, opts) end

--- A view that draws cells, at a size in pixels: its snapshot
--- (`paw.api.ui_view_snapshot`) as runs of text, one style for each run.
--- paw draws it again only after a request for a redraw. It also draws it
--- one time each second for the parts that change by themselves (times),
--- and on each call while the song plays. The result is `{ same = true }` when the drawing is
--- the same as the last drawing. `window` keeps the last drawing. The view
--- in a separate window has a line below it for prompts, messages and the
--- keys of the view. This is not so when the frontend draws a status line
--- there itself (`opts.status`). paw sends the windows of a graphical
--- frontend as scenes (`paw.ui.render_scene`).
---@param name any
---@param window any
---@param width any
---@param height any
---@param opts any
function paw.canvas.paint_cells(name, window, width, height, opts) end

--- Show the picture `key` over the cells `x, y, w, h` of `buf`: see
--- `buf:image`. In a terminal that shows images and in a window, it is a
--- strip (`buf:strip`) under the cells, and the result is true; elsewhere
--- half blocks, two pixels a cell.
---@param buf any
---@param x any
---@param y any
---@param w any
---@param h any
---@param key any
function paw.canvas.place(buf, x, y, w, h, key) end

--- Pass a pointer event from a frontend to a view, which then has the
--- keyboard. It goes to its `pointer` function in pixels. For a view in
--- cells, it goes to its `on_mouse` function, as from the terminal.
---@param name any
---@param event any
function paw.canvas.pointer(name, event) end

--- True if a graphical frontend shows a view. The view is in a separate
--- window, or docked where the screen of the seat is the main area of a
--- frontend. That is a frontend that told its size to `paw.panes`, or the
--- frontend of the instance when there is no terminal.
---@param name any
function paw.canvas.shown(name) end

--- The width of a line of text in pixels, near enough, at `size` (12).
---@param text any
---@param size any
function paw.canvas.text_width(text, size) end

--- Give a pointer event in pixels to a painted view: to its hit list
--- (`view.hits`) first, then to its `pointer` function.
---@param view any
---@param e any
function paw.canvas.view_pointer(view, e) end

--- The windows that are open, for a frontend: `{ name, title, width, height, cells }`
--- each, `cells` for a view drawn as the terminal draws it.
function paw.canvas.windows() end

--- Add an empty branch to the split `split`, as one undo step:
--- `opts.name`, and `opts.index` (from 1; the end when nil).
---@param h any
---@param split any
---@param opts any
function paw.chain.add_branch(h, split, opts) end

--- Add a macro named `name` to the split `split`, as one undo step.
---@param h any
---@param split any
---@param name any
function paw.chain.add_macro(h, split, name) end

--- Change the linked rack to what the instance `split` is now: each other
--- instance follows, and keeps its own values.
---@param h any
---@param split any
function paw.chain.apply_rack(h, split) end

--- Let a chain have a split of a new kind, for an extension: `spec` gives
--- the fewest (`min`) and the most (`max`) branches, and how many a new
--- split of the kind gets (`first`). The name is words joined by dots,
--- such as `drums.transient`. A kind of paw cannot be defined again.
---@param name any
---@param spec any
function paw.chain.define_kind(name, spec) end

--- The split, the branch or the macro with the id `id` in `chain`, and
--- the splits around it. Nil when the chain does not have it.
---@param chain any
---@param id any
function paw.chain.find(chain, id) end

--- The chain of the owner `h`, or nil when it has none: then its devices
--- play in series, in the order of the owner's list.
---@param h any
function paw.chain.get(h) end

--- The names of the kinds of split: `chain`, `layer`, `band`, `ms`, `lr`,
--- `key`, `vel`, `select`, `outs`, and the kinds that extensions defined.
function paw.chain.kinds() end

--- Link the macro `macro` to the parameter `param` of `device` in its
--- split, with `depth` from -1 to 1; a nil depth removes the link. One
--- undo step.
---@param h any
---@param macro any
---@param device any
---@param param any
---@param depth any
function paw.chain.link(h, macro, device, param, depth) end

--- Make the split `split` the first instance of a new linked rack named
--- `name`: a change of the rack in one instance reaches the others.
---@param h any
---@param split any
---@param name any
function paw.chain.link_rack(h, split, name) end

--- Move the node `id` in its chain, as one undo step: `{ before = id }`,
--- `{ after = id }` or `{ branch = id, index = n }` (from 1).
---@param h any
---@param id any
---@param place any
function paw.chain.move(h, id, place) end

--- The handle of the chain of `owner`, which each other function of
--- paw.chain takes: `{ track = id }` for the chain of a track. An error
--- when the owner is not in the project.
---@param owner any
function paw.chain.of(owner) end

--- The keys of the values that the instance `split` overrides, as
--- `paw.chain.set_rack` takes them.
---@param h any
---@param split any
function paw.chain.overrides(h, split) end

--- Put a new instance of the linked rack `rack` at `place` in the chain
--- of `h` (as `paw.chain.move` takes it).
---@param h any
---@param rack any
---@param place any
function paw.chain.place_rack(h, rack, place) end

--- The linked racks of the project.
function paw.chain.racks() end

--- Remove what `id` names, as one undo step: a split or a branch with its
--- devices, a macro with its links, or a device.
---@param h any
---@param id any
function paw.chain.remove(h, id) end

--- Give the instance `split` each value of its linked rack.
---@param h any
---@param split any
function paw.chain.reset_rack(h, split) end

paw.chain.say = paw.notify

--- Change a split (`name`, `mix`, `gain`, `kind`, `values`), a branch
--- (`name`, `gain`, `pan`, `mute`, `solo`, `zone`) or a macro (`name`,
--- `value`), as one undo step. A new `kind` keeps the branches.
---@param h any
---@param id any
---@param patch any
function paw.chain.set(h, id, patch) end

--- Change a value of the linked rack `rack` by its key, in each instance
--- that does not override it. The key is a reference of the chain
--- language with an id of the rack's definition: `"#<id>.gain"` for a
--- split, a branch or a macro, `"#<id>.<param>"` for a parameter of a
--- device.
---@param rack any
---@param key any
---@param value any
function paw.chain.set_rack(rack, key, value) end

--- Let `device` take its key from the node `from` of its chain, from
--- `"in"` (the input of the chain), or (nil) from no node of the chain.
--- One undo step.
---@param h any
---@param device any
---@param from any
function paw.chain.tap(h, device, from) end

--- Turn the instance `split` into a copy that follows no linked rack.
---@param h any
---@param split any
function paw.chain.unlink_rack(h, split) end

--- Take the split `split` away, as one undo step. With `keep` (a branch
--- of it), that branch takes its place and the devices of the other
--- branches leave the owner; without, its branches go in its place one
--- after the other.
---@param h any
---@param split any
---@param keep any
function paw.chain.unwrap(h, split, keep) end

--- Wrap the nodes `nodes` (devices or splits in a row of one branch) of
--- the chain of `h` in a new split of `kind`, as one undo step. They go
--- into its first branch. `opts.branches` gives how many branches (the
--- kind's first count when nil), `opts.name` its name, `opts.values`
--- values of its kind (`{ xover = { 120, 2000 } }` for a band split).
---@param h any
---@param nodes any
---@param kind any
---@param opts any
function paw.chain.wrap(h, nodes, kind, opts) end

--- The chords to offer at the pencil, in this order: the diatonic triads
--- and sevenths of `key`, then the common qualities on the pitch `near`.
--- Each with its symbol, name, numeral and pitches.
---@param key any
---@param near any
function paw.chords.offer(key, near) end

--- Read a chord symbol in `key` (paw.harmony's key, C major when nil)
--- with its root nearest the pitch `near` (60 when nil). Returns the chord,
--- or nil and why not.
---@param text any
---@param key any
---@param near any
function paw.chords.parse(text, key, near) end

--- Add a chord name: its suffix after the root (`"7#9"`) and its tones
--- in semitones above the root. A name the reading does not know is
--- written but not read.
---@param suffix any
---@param steps any
---@param name any
function paw.chords.quality(suffix, steps, name) end

--- The chord of `n` notes (3 a triad, 4 a seventh) on `root`, a pitch,
--- stacked in thirds from the scale of `key`: the diatonic chord.
---@param root any
---@param n any
---@param key any
function paw.chords.stack(root, n, key) end

--- A new clip of the kind `name` on `track`: `data`, or what the kind's
--- `create` makes of `args` (the words, and `name`, `start` and `length`
--- of the clip). Its length is `args.length`, or the length the kind
--- compiled, or a bar of 4 beats. One undo step.
---@param track any
---@param name any
---@param args any
---@param data any
function paw.clip_kinds.add(track, name, args, data) end

--- Compile a clip of a kind again from its data (after something that it
--- links to changed). One undo step.
---@param clip any
function paw.clip_kinds.compile(clip) end

--- The data of a clip of a kind, by its kind's `deserialize`: nil for a
--- MIDI or audio clip, and nil and why when the kind cannot read it.
--- Without its kind, the plain data as it is stored.
---@param clip any
function paw.clip_kinds.data(clip) end

--- Define a kind of clip (see above). `name` is a lowercase word that is
--- not a built-in kind (`midi`, `audio`): what a clip of the kind keeps.
--- Defining a kind again replaces it. Clips of an older version of its
--- data are migrated.
---@param name any
---@param spec any
function paw.clip_kinds.define(name, spec) end

--- A few words on a clip of a kind, for `K`: `Film clip · intro.mp4`, or
--- "film (no extension here defines it)". Nil for a MIDI or audio clip.
---@param clip any
function paw.clip_kinds.describe(clip) end

--- The names of the defined kinds, in the order they were defined.
function paw.clip_kinds.list() end

--- How a clip of a kind looks in the arrange view, by its kind's `look`:
--- nil without the kind, or when `look` fails (said once per kind).
---@param clip any
---@param ctx any
function paw.clip_kinds.look(clip, ctx) end

--- Move the clips of the kind `name` (of each kind when nil) whose data is
--- of an older version to their kind's version, by its `deserialize`, and
--- compile them again. Says how many moved; a clip whose migration fails
--- stays as it was, with a message. Gives how many moved.
---@param name any
function paw.clip_kinds.migrate(name) end

--- Forget a kind: no new clips of it. The clips of it that the project
--- has stay as they are, data and all, until something defines it again.
---@param name any
function paw.clip_kinds.remove(name) end

--- Give a clip of a kind new data: the kind checks it and compiles it
--- again, and the data, sound, signals and files change together, as one
--- undo step. Refused without the kind, and for a clip that a newer
--- version of the kind made.
---@param clip any
---@param data any
function paw.clip_kinds.set(clip, data) end

--- What a clip of a kind holds when what it plays moves `by` beats later
--- under it: by the kind's `slip` (given the new offset), or the same data
--- with its offset moved.
---@param clip any
---@param by any
function paw.clip_kinds.slip(clip, by) end

--- The kind `name` as defined, or nil. `D` is the record of its data.
---@param name any
function paw.clip_kinds.spec(name) end

--- What the two halves of a clip of a kind hold when it is split `at`
--- beats after its start, as `paw.api.clip_set_custom` takes it: by the
--- kind's `split`, or (without it, or without the kind) the same data,
--- with the right half starting where the cut is in it. The caller makes
--- the halves.
---@param clip any
---@param at any
function paw.clip_kinds.split(clip, at) end

--- What a clip of a kind holds when it is cut to the beats `from` to `to`
--- after its start: by the kind's `trim`, or the same data with its
--- offset moved to `from`. The caller moves the clip's start and length.
---@param clip any
---@param from any
---@param to any
function paw.clip_kinds.trim(clip, from, to) end

--- Make `alias` another name for `target`.
---@param alias any
---@param target any
function paw.command.alias(alias, target) end

--- Define or replace a command.
---   paw.command("track.mute", function(ctx) ... end, { desc = "Mute the track" })
--- The handler gets `ctx` with `ctx.args` (positional and named arguments)
--- and `ctx.count` (the number typed before a key mapping, or nil). With
--- `opts.raw`, the command line gives its text as one string in
--- `ctx.args[1]`. With `opts.range`, it accepts a range of bars before it
--- (`:5,8name`) as `ctx.range`. See Ranges below.
---@param name any
---@param run any
---@param opts any
function paw.command.define(name, run, opts) end

--- Run a command line, as typed after `:`, with a range in front if it has one.
---@param line any
---@param ctx any
function paw.command.exec(line, ctx) end

--- The command of a name: `{ name, run, desc, ... }` as `paw.command` made it, or nil.
---@param name any
function paw.command.get(name) end

--- Every command, sorted by name.
function paw.command.list() end

--- The extension that paw loads now, or whose code registers these commands
--- and keys later. `:help` goes to its page for them, and an unload removes them.
function paw.command.owner() end

paw.command.run = paw.cmd

--- A command that can be adjusted says the range it changed, as the panel
--- of `<leader>.` shows it (`Quantize · 48 notes`). Call it from the handler.
---   paw.commands.about("48 notes")
---@param text any
function paw.commands.about(text) end

--- Run the last command that has `adjust` again with other parameters, in
--- place of its undo step: `paw.commands.adjust({ grid = "1/8" })`, or
--- positional ones, by the order of its `adjust`. The project goes back to
--- before the command, the command runs again with its first parameters
--- and these laid over them, and the undo step is the new result. It
--- works only on the last change: after `undo`, or another change, it says so.
---@param given any
function paw.commands.adjust(given) end

--- The line `adjust a=1 b=2` that holds the parameters of the last
--- command that can be adjusted, as they are now; nil when no command ran
--- that can be adjusted. `<leader>.` puts it on the command line.
function paw.commands.adjusting() end

paw.commands.alias = paw.command.alias

--- What could finish a command line: a list of `{ text, label, desc }` with
--- `text` the whole line it would become and `label` the part shown.
---   paw.commands.complete("track.co")   --> { { text = "track.color", label = "track.color", desc = ... } }
---@param line any
function paw.commands.complete(line) end

paw.commands.define = paw.command.define

paw.commands.exec = paw.command.exec

paw.commands.get = paw.command.get

--- What an undo step made by a command is called: its `label`, or the
--- start of its description, before any example or alternative.
---@param spec any
function paw.commands.label(spec) end

paw.commands.list = paw.command.list

--- What a bare extension name (`:notation`) stands for: its `.open` or
--- `.show` command, where it has one.
